## 1. Introduction
Sustainable energy is the basic need for a nation to be economically stable without compromising the quality of an environment. The use of large amount of fossil fuels to cater the energy needs of the society all around the globe is ensuing to serious environmental issues and also the depletion of natural resources. According to British Petroleum (BP) Statistical Review of World Energy 2019 $\mathrm{\hbar}^{\mathsf{\prime}}68^{\mathrm{{th}}}$ edition), primary energy consumption in 2018 extended to 13,865 million tonnes oil equivalent with total carbon emissions 33,891 million tonnes. A high growth in the global energy consumption of $2.8\%$ in 2018 compared to the previous years resulted into a significant rise $(2\%)$ in global carbon emissions [1]. However, with a significant improvement in 2019, primary energy consumption rose by $1.3\%$ with a decline in global carbon emissions by $0.5\%$ according to British Petroleum (BP) Statistical Review of World Energy 2020 $69^{\mathrm{th}}$ edition) [2]. This reduction in the total carbon emissions was due to a major contribution of the renewable resources to meet the uprising energy requirements with a deprivation of demands for coal and oil for energy production. However, in 2020, the COVID-19 pandemic had the startling impact on energy markets with a fall in primary energy consumption by $4.5\%$ and a decline in total carbon emissions by $6.3\%$ . This remarkable decline in the total carbon emissions is due to the decrease in the global energy demands with an increase in the renewable energy demands according to British Petroleum (BP) Statistical Review of World Energy 2021 ( $70^{\mathrm{th}}$ edition) [3].
Among various renewable energy resources, Sun is an ultimate source that fulfils the energy needs of all forms of life on earth. The generation of chemical energy by plants in the presence of sunlight exists as the oldest and the most proficient chemical process for the effective use of solar energy. The well-known process of photosynthesis involves the breaking of stable water molecules into molecular oxygen on the surface of chlorophyll. The great inspiration from nature has fueled research in the use of solar energy for water splitting to generate molecular hydrogen that offers an excellent source of clean, sustainable and renewable energy [4- 6]. The strategy of photocatalytic water splitting is amenable to provide a sustainable solution to the multiplying energy needs and environmental crisis. However, the practical photo splitting of hard hitting water molecules presents a real challenge because the redox potential for the desired reaction is $+0.81\backslash$ V with respect to NHE with a positive change in Gibb’s free energy $\langle\Delta\mathsf{G}=+237\left|\mathsf{k J}\right|$ - mol) [7,8]. Thus, making the thermodynamically uphill reaction feasible, a semiconducting photocatalyst is required. The activity of the photocatalyst in the visible region is among the important eligibility factors as $43\%$ content of the sunlight reaching the earth surface is visible light and only $4\%$ of sunlight contains UV light [9]. The mechanism for hydrogen production using semiconductor photocatalyst involves the absorption of sunlight by that material to excite electrons from valence band to conduction band with the holes remaining at valence band giving rise to the separation of charges. These charges when reach the surface of the catalyst, the protons from water splitting accept electrons to reduce themselves to hydrogen molecules, and the holes, on combination with water molecules generate oxygen molecules. The theoretical optical band gap for water splitting reactions, corresponding to Gibb’s free energy $\langle\Delta\mathrm{G}=+237~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\rangle$ is $1.23\mathrm{eV}$ . The water splitting reactions are composed of two half reactions viz. hydrogen evolution reaction (1; $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}=$ proton and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}=$ hole) and oxygen evolution reaction $\mathbf{\bar{H}}^{+}=$ proton and $\mathbf{e^{-}}=$ electron) (Fig. 1) [9-11].
Oxidation half reaction (oxygen evolution reaction).
$$
2h^{+}+H_{2}O\rightarrow1/2O_{2}+2H^{+}
$$
Reduction half reaction (hydrogen evolution reaction).
$$
2H^{+}+2e^{-}\rightarrow H_{2}
$$
Overall water splitting reaction.
$$
H_{2}O\rightarrow H_{2}+1/2O_{2}
$$
After the discovery of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by Fujishima and Honda as the first photocatalyst for the production of hydrogen from water in 1972 [12], unflagging efforts have been made in designing various inorganic and organic semiconducting materials such as metal oxides [13,14], metal nitrides [15], carbon nitrides [16,17], metal organic frameworks [18], covalent organic frameworks [19,20] etc. for the efficient photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. However, for the practical scale applications, the development of materials with the enhanced photocatalytic efficiency remains desirable.
In recent years, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derivatives have emerged as an attractive class of materials with unique features and potential for a variety of applications [21– 29]. MOFs are crystalline porous materials that are formed by the strong bonds between multivalent metal nodes (Lewis acid) and hetero atom donor containing organic linkers (Lewis base) [30- 32]. These contain homometallic building units that provide crystallinity to these frameworks without achieving any amorphous phase during their reticular synthesis. These materials have received much attention due to their distinctive rigid 3-D structures that can be tuned by varying the nature of metal precursors as well as the organic linkers pertaining to the targeted applications [18,33-37]. After the first report of photoactive $\scriptstyle{\big[}{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}({\mathsf{p}}-$ $\mathsf{B D C})_{2}\mathbf{\bar{l}}_{\mathrm{n}}$ MOF for the water splitting reactions by Mori et al. in 2009 [38], the research in exploration of MOFs for the water splitting is continuously expanding towards searching the catalysts with high photocatalytic performance for this process. Several materials in this class have been found as proficient photocatalysts for hydrogen generation from water splitting reactions. Various factors that offer these materials as potent photocatalysts for such processes include, (1) presence of organic components make the coordination architectures to be responsive to external light resulting into their high efficiency to harness sunlight in UV/visible region in order to excite electrons from HOMO to LUMO, (2) high porosity and large BET surface area offers the possibility of loading photoactive complexes or dyes or co-catalysts into the pores for the enhanced photoactivity, (3) the long charge separation decay (in microsecond) in these materials provides sufficient time for the desired reaction to happen on the catalytic surface, (4) photo, chemical, mechanical and thermal stabilities of the MOFs can help in their recyclability leading to the low-cost photocatalytic hydrogen production from water splitting reactions [39-44].
Despite several advantages, majority of the homometallic MOFs reported for hydrogen production suffer from the limitations of their activity in UV light and low catalytic efficiency. However, heterogeneity can be devised into these frameworks by introducing either the different metallic building units or the organic linkers in which the functionalities can be tuned according to the specific application. Mixed metal-metal organic frameworks (MM-MOFs) are the class of MOFs that contain two or more metal ions anywhere in the framework, and endow these with an added degree of structural diversity and a variety of innovative applications [45-53]. The concept was first introduced by Chen and coworkers in 2011 where they explained that immobilization of functional metal sites or open metal catalytic sites within the metal organic frameworks improves their applicative properties by promoting metal–ligand or metal–metal synergism [54]. These heterogeneous systems have two or more metal ions either at the nodal position; or one metal ion at nodal position and other at satellite nodal position. These metal ions within the frameworks show synergistic interactions with each other to enhance the applicative properties than their homometallic parts. However, metals ions, metal complexes, organometallic complexes or metal compounds as guests loaded MOFs show enhancement in applicative properties than their homometallic parts because of synergistic interactions between loaded compounds and MOFs at the interface of the hybrid system. Generally, these hybrid systems do not follow the definition of MM-MOFs but can be studied along with MM-MOFs because synergistic interactions which leads to the enhanced applicative properties are observed in both the heterogeneous systems [55-57].
MM-MOFs have attracted a great consideration in recent years for water splitting reactions because, (1) the introduction of two metal ions causes synergistic interaction between the metal ions which helps in reducing the optical band gap for the desired reaction, (2) introduction of photoactive or metal-containing organic linker makes the frameworks visible light active and improves the properties compared to the homometallic MOFs, (3) presence of two metal ions greatly helps in reducing the recombination of photo-generated electrons and holes, as one of the metal ions behaves as an electron sink and helps in transferring electrons to the protons for the generation of molecular hydrogen.
A few review articles previously reported on MM-MOFs provided insights into the applications of these materials in catalysis, organic transformation reactions, gas adsorption and separation etc. [46- 48,58] The present review focuses on the synthetic approaches of the MM-MOFs and their applications in photocatalytic production of hydrogen from water splitting reactions. A timeline for the development of MM-MOFs and their applications in photocatalytic hydrogen generation from water splitting reactions is presented in Fig. 2. Exploration of various synthesis approaches is directed towards the enhancement of photocatalytic properties of the materials through the achievement of synergistic interactions between metal ions and/or incorporation of photo active groups.
To the best of our knowledge, this is the first review to classify reported MM-MOFs for photocatalytic hydrogen production from water splitting reactions on the basis of their synthetic technique. Along with this, synthetic approaches for metal/metal complex/ metal compound loaded MOFs have been discussed with their applications in photocatalytic hydrogen production from water splitting reactions.
## 2. Synthetic approaches for MM-MOFs
Homometallic MOFs with organic linkers are well reported in the literature and most of them are synthesized via hydro/- solvothermal, slow evaporation, sonication and mechanochemical methods [59-62]. The chemical stability of the homometallic MOFs is the first and the foremost criteria for synthesizing and studying them for the desirable applications which initially depends upon the type of interaction between a metal ion and an organic linker as explained by Leus et al. [48] According to Pearson’s hard/soft acid/base (HSAB) principle, high valent metal ions (hard Lewis acid) make stable interactions with organic linkers containing carboxylate ions (hard Lewis base). This can be viewed as high charge density on metal ion which enhances the covalency of the bond between metal ion and organic linkers and make the system stable towards various external stimuli such as hydrolysis, temperature, pH etc. [63]. Also, metal ions with low charge density (soft Lewis acid) make stable interactions with azolate linkers (soft Lewis base) according to HSAB rule. In order to establish a structure– property relationship, MOFs are activated after their synthesis. However, certain MOF structures are deformed and the materials become mechanically tender during the activation process because of high surface tension and strong capillary forces of polar solvents such as DMF and water. This can be avoided by the replacement of high surface tension polar solvents with the low boiling and less polar solvents using solvent exchange experiment during the activation process [30].
Thus, the strategies used for synthesis of stable homometallic MOFs, can in principle also be applied to obtain the stable MMMOFs. However, synthesis of MM-MOFs with desired structures and compositions is a challenge as creating metal vacancies, metal excess and varying the pore sizes without disturbing the lattice order and crystallinity is a tedious task. Further, the incorporation of second metal ion into the frameworks increases the chances of the other metal ion to occupy the satellite nodal position resulting into the formation of capricious topology and brittle frameworks. Synthesis of heterogeneous metal organic frameworks relies on various factors that includes: (i) similar chemical behaviour and ionic radii of metal ions, (ii) same coulombic charge on the metal ions, and (iii) Lewis basicity of the organic linkers (usually O-, Ndonor linkers). These factors help in uniform incorporation of the second metal ions into the frameworks and stronger bond formation between the metal ions and organic linkers. Thus, design of MM-MOFs requires judicious choice of metal centers, organic linkers, sequence of addition of metals and organic linkers as well as the reaction parameters such as solvent, temperature, pH etc. [64-66].
In the literature, synthetic approaches for MM-MOFs are broadly classified into (a) one-pot synthesis, and (b) postsynthetic modification. These involve the use of different metal salts as metal precursors, and suitable organic linkers that react to form the bi- or tri-metallic coordination polymers. Various synthetic approaches used for MM-MOFs reported in the literature are summarized in Table 1.
Various analytical techniques can be used for the characterization of MM-MOFs that provide information about the important parameters such as distribution of metal ions, molecular structures, composition and morphology of the frameworks. Crystallinity of the MM-MOFs is studied using X-ray diffraction (XRD) and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) techniques [67]. It is known from Vegard’s law that diffraction peaks of the MM-MOFs lie in between the peaks of homometallic MOFs obtained from the respective individual metals. The distribution of metal ions and their concentration can be studied by using atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS), inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES), coupled EDX mapping and aerosol timeof-flight mass spectrometry (ATOF-MS) [68,69]. Advanced characterization techniques such as extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) can be used to study the location of metal ions in the frameworks to confirm whether the metal ions occupy the nodal sites or the voids. X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES), neutron powder diffraction (NPD) are used to analyze the environment of metal ions such as average coordination number, bond length etc. [70].
## 2.1. One-pot synthesis
Several MM-MOFs have been reported to be synthesized by directly reacting the appropriate mixture of metal precursors with the organic linker (Fig. 3). This approach is based on the classical methods that are commonly used for the synthesis of MOFs such as reactions by conventional heating or the use of solvothermal reactors. The use of different metal ions with similar chemical properties and electrical charge can provide crystallinity to the frameworks by avoiding the non-preferable metal ions going into the amorphous portion of the solid material. Also, such metal ions show more synergistic interaction with each other [71,72].
A variety of MM-MOFs have been prepared using the one-pot synthetic strategy. Cobalt and Nickel based bimetallic organic frameworks were synthesized using $\mathsf{N i S O}_{4}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ $6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ as metal precursors, and benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) and 2-amino-benzenedicarboxylic acid ( $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC) as organic linkers in N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) under solvothermal conditions. EDX spectrum of the (Co-Ni) MM-MOF confirmed that metal ions were uniformly distributed in the framework [73]. A similar synthetic approach was followed by Zhou et al. for the synthesis of $\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{F e}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M O F}{-}74$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{x}}$ is molar ratio of Co) using $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{-}$ $.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ as metal precursors, 2,5- dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\mathrm{^{\prime}H_{4}D O B D C)}$ as an organic linker in a mixture of DMF, deionized water and ethanol (Fig. 4). Among the MM-MOFs synthesized using various molar concentrations of metal ions, Co0.74Fe0.26-MOF-74 showed the best performance in hydrogen evolution reactions. Morphology of $\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{F e}_{1-\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathsf{M O F}{-}74$ was studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and compared with the homometallic Co-MOF-74. The SEM images of Co-MOF-74 showed prismatic bars which stacked up into microflowers with diameter and length of each bar of about 5–10 and $20\mathrm{-}40~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively (Fig. 5a). With the introduction of Fe, new microflowers for ${\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathrm{x}}{\mathsf{F e}}_{1-\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{-MOF}}{\mathrm{-}}74$ were obtained with a diameter reduced to $2{-}5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 5b, c). Interestingly, upon increasing the Fe content to 0.31 the microflower structure disappeared and the prismatic bars were converted to the nanoparticles in $\mathrm{Co}_{0.69}\mathrm{Fe}_{0.31}$ -MOF-74 (Fig. 5d). The morphology of Fe-MOF-74 displayed single bars with the lengths $8-15~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (the inset of Fig. 5d) [74].
Mostly, a mixture of different metals is generally used to achieve the heterogeneity in the MM-MOFs, however, the mixed ligands can also be used to introduce the structural heterogeneity and also to tune the flexibility or rigidity of the frameworks as per the requirement. One such approach used by Shi et al. involved the synthesis of a microporous framework of sodium and copper. The cost effective mixed-metals $(\mathsf{N a}/\mathsf{C u})$ mixed-ligands (flexible/rigid) metal–organic framework $_{\mathrm{Na/Cu-MOF}}$ was obtained by the direct solvothermal synthesis using $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C l O}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{10}[\propto-\mathrm{SiW}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{34}]{\cdot}18\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as metal precursors in citric acid-sodium citrate buffer solutions in acetonitrile-methanol mixture (Fig. 6) [75].
