In MM-MOFs, synergism can be improved by modulating MMMOFs through alloying metal ions. In one such study, Lin et al. developed Fe- and Ni- based MM-MOF, namely FeNi (BDC)(DMF, F), grown over the surface of nickel foam. This resulted into the formation of two well mixed bi-metallic MOF phases, Fe-rich FeNi (BDC)(DMF, F) and Ni-rich FeNi(BDC)(F) which showed hierarchical synergistic effects in the electrocatalytic applications. In a typical procedure, BDC and a mixture of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\mathsf{N i C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ in DMF were transferred to the autoclave charged with pretreated Ni foam (Fig. 8) and the MOFs were allowed to grow over the surface of Ni solvothermally [77].
Serre et al. synthesized a breathable MIL-53(Cr/Fe) MM-MOF using a stoichiometric concentration of chromium nitrate and iron as metal precursors, BDC as an organic linker in an aqueous hydrofluoric acid (HF). They observed the reversible contraction and expansion in the framework on applying external stimuli such as temperature, pressure and the interactions with certain guest molecules [78].
It is noteworthy that the metal ions with electronic and chemical similarities can afford stable and crystalline MM-MOFs via a one-pot synthetic approach. The lack of such similarity in the metal ions may lead to the fickle geometries. This can be avoided by using the alternative approaches such as the use of metalloligands as starting materials and the post synthetic structural modifications. These strategies can be effective in achieving heterogeneous frameworks from electronically and chemically different metal ions with no loss in the molecular stability and crystallinity.
## 2.2. Use of metalloligand precursors
In this approach, a metal that shows more affinity with the organic ligand is developed into a metalloligand. This metalloligand can be used directly with the mixture of another metal ion and an organic linker to obtain the MM-MOF. This is one of the most convenient strategies to immobilize metal clusters and develop such porous structures. In addition, since the metal complexes are immobilized in the frameworks, agglomeration of metal complexes leading to the deactivation of MM-MOFs can be avoided. Although highly convenient, the method is associated with a limitation that one of the metal ions occupies nodal position and the other one occupies the satellite nodal position which may make the structures brittle (Fig. 9) [79,80].
In an alternative process, metalloligands are used to synthesize MM-MOFs via two-step synthetic process where the first step involves the formation of a homogeneous MOF, which in the second step undergoes the replacement of organic linker with the metalloligand [81,82].
Various Ru-, Ir-, Pt-, porphyrin based metalloligands are reported to be incorporated into the MOF structures to obtain MM-MOFs via one-step or two steps synthetic procedures.
$\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{t p y})_{2}$ complex was successfully incorporated into a Ti-based MOF solvothermally using tetrapropyl orthotitanate (TPOT) in DMF-MeOH solvent mixture via one-pot synthesis [83]. In another report, similar Ru- based complex was solvothermally mixed with $\mathsf{M n}(\mathsf{C l O}_{4})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , or $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ to afford single crystals of $\mathrm{Mn}_{0.5}[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{tpyCOO})_{2}]\mathrm{ClO}_{4},$ (denoted as Mn Ru), $\mathsf{C o}_{0.5}[\mathsf{R u}$ $(\mathrm{tpyCOO})_{2}]\mathrm{PF}_{6},$ , (denoted as ${\bf C o}-{\bf R}{\bf u})$ ), and $Z\mathrm{n}_{0.5}[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{tpyCOO})_{2}]\mathrm{PF}_{6},$ (denoted as Zn-Ru), respectively in a one-step reaction [84].
Song et al. reported Ir- and Ru-embedded MM-MOFs, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDCIr and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ -BPDC-Ru by heating $\left[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{d c b p y})\right]^{+}$ and $[\mathsf{R u(b p y)}_{2}(-$ dcbpy) $]^{2+}(\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}$ -dipyridyl; ppy $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ pyridyl; dcbpy $=2{,}2{^{\prime}}$ -bipyri dine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid), respectively with $\mathrm{Ti_{6}O_{6}(O i P r)_{6}(a b z)_{6}}$ ( $\mathtt{a b z}=4$ -aminobenzoate), $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b p d c}$ in DMF at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ solvothermally [85]. In a similar study, Yang et al. subjected the mixture of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ , $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl dicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bpdc}}\right)$ , $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , Ru(dcbpy) $(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}$ to one-pot solvothermal reaction in DMF to obtain Ru-PtUIO-67 MM-MOF (Fig. 10) [125].
A Ce-based $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $\cdot5,5^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid (bpydc) metalloligand was successfully incorporated into $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF, UiO67 using $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpdc as an organic linker with 0.02 Ce to Zr atom ratio in a one-step reaction [87].
Incorporation of catalytically relevant precious metals such as Ru, Pt, Pd, Ir as SBUs into the MOF systems is a challenge because of kinetic inertness of these metals. Lan et al. synthesized Ru-TBPZn MOF using $\mathtt{R u}_{2}$ SBU; porphyrin based Zn-containing $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBP-Zn}$ metalloligand in DMF at $120{\sf C}$ for two days via one pot solvothermal process [88].
Alternatively, metalloligands are intercalated into the structures of MOF system via ligand exchange approach in two-step process. Liao et al. incorporated Ru- and Rh- based complexes viz $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{R u O H}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{R u C l}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{R h C l}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{RhOH}_{2}$ into UiO-67 by immersing the MOF into the DMF solution of the complexes for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 11) [69]. Kim et al. synthesized bipyridine-embedded UiOtype MOF using $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ as a metal precursor, dcbpy as an organic linker, and $\left[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{d c b p y})\right]^{+}$ as metalloligand in DMF using solvothermal method in first step to afford Ir_BUiO and then, Pt $(\mathsf{d c b p y})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ was incorporated by stirring the solution containing Ir_BUiO and $\mathrm{Pt}.$ -complex to afford $\mathsf{P t}_{0.1}$ _Ir_BUiO [89].
## 2.3. Post modification
Post modification approach has been found to be successful to introduce heterogeneity into the metal organic frameworks to improve their applicative properties. The reported various post modification approaches involve the exchange of metal ions from the nodal sites of the frameworks; growth of one type of MOF over other MOF. The loading amount of the second or the third metal ion into the frameworks can be controlled to tune the chemical and physical properties of the MM-MOFs for the desirable application. This synthetic technique depends upon various factors such as MOF lattice, ionic radii and valency of metal ion, and the denticity of the organic linker.
## 2.3.1. Core-shell method
A MOF can be epitaxially grown on the surface of another MOF. In this method, MOF of one type of metal ion may behave as a core and can be used as a seed to grow the MOF of another metal like a shell over it. Different Metal ions may have different reactivity with the same organic linker to develop a framework. Using this information, whenever, two dissimilar metal ions with different reactivity are allowed to react with an organic linker, one of the metal ions develops a framework that behaves as a core for the other metal ion whose reactivity is less with the linker (Fig. 12) [90].
Orr et al. synthesized $z_{\mathrm{{n-}}}$ and Cd-based core–shell MM-MOF using 2-methyl imidazole $\mathrm{{(mIm)}}$ as an organic linker. They studied various samples of $\mathrm{Zn_{x}C d_{1-x}(m I m)_{2}}$ at various molar concentrations of metal ions $(0\leq\mathbf{x}\leq1),$ within the temperature ranges from $20^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . It was found that ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ ions has lower charge density than $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions which makes them more reactive with mIm to produce the core MOF that provides its surface for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion to grow as a shell. With the increase in the molar concentration of the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ ion, the tendency to develop core–shell MM-MOF was increased [90].
## 2.3.2. Metal removal-addition
MM-MOFs can also be obtained by removing the metal ions from the frameworks using elimination reactions and healing them with another metal ion using addition reactions (Fig. 13).
Nguyen et al. metallated OH group of the Zr based MOF UiO-66 with $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}^{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}}$ metal ions to make more stable VUiO-66 MM-MOF over various temperature ranges. The UiO-66 obtained from the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ in DMF was further activated with ${\mathsf{V O}}({\mathsf{a c a c}})_{2}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ to furnish VUiO-66 framework (Fig. 14) [91].
## 2.3.3. Metal replacement (transmetalation)
Direct Partial replacement of metal ions in the homometallic MOFs with another metal ion has also been explored for the synthesis of MM-MOFs. In this method, the homometallic MOF is immersed into the solution of other metal ions. In this method, the metal-exchange process occurs at a significant rate if there is a well-established reversibility of the metal–ligand bonds. This method is behind the construction of MOFs with more crystallinity (behind the increase in crystallinity of MOFs) (Fig. 15) [92].
A Co-doped Ni-MOF-74 was obtained by synthesizing Ni-MOF74 first and then, immersing it into the solution of cobalt precursor. Ni-MOF-74 was obtained by heating the mixture of $_{\mathrm{H}_{4}D O B D C}$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , in a mixed solvent containing DMF, ethanol and water in an autoclave. Multiple immersions of the MOF in the DMF solution of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ at $80~^{\circ}C$ afforded the desired MM-MOF (Fig. 16) [93].
## 3. Metal, metal complex and metal compound loaded MOFs
MOFs have high specific surface areas and large active sites which allows to disperse inorganic semiconductor metal compounds (metal oxides, metal sulfides, metal phosphides), metal NPs, metal complexes or organometallic complexes to disperse on the frameworks through the interactions between loaded metal ions and functionalities of the framework or through metal nodes of the MOFs. In such frameworks, although the loaded metal does not constitute a part of the framework, the presence of second metal causes the synergistic interactions leading to the enhanced photo activities of the MOFs. Although, this class of materials have not yet received a MM-MOF nomenclature, it is worth mentioning this class of frameworks because of their enhanced photocatalytic activities due to the synergism between the metal centres [94-98].
## 3.1. Metal and metal complex loading
This involves the loading of a metal or a metal complex as a guest into the framework of a MOF. The presence of certain free functionalities in the organic linkers of homometallic MOFs provide sites for their interaction with the guest metals or metal complexes leading to the formation of inclusion frameworks. This can be done by soaking the MOF in a solution of metal ion or metal complex to be loaded into the framework. After the loading of metal ions into MOF structures, metal nano-particles inside the framework can be obtained by reduction using suitable reducing reagents or photo/electro-reduction (Fig. 17) [99,100].
## 3.1.1. Metal complex loading
Various metal complexes such as hydrogenase, polyoxometallates, Ni- and $C0-$ based metal complexes have successfully been incorporated into the MOFs. Kong et al. encapsulated Nicontaining POM $[\mathsf{N i}_{4}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{P W}_{9}\mathsf{O}_{34})_{2}]^{10_{-}}(\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{P}_{2})$ into $[\mathsf{R u(b p y)}_{2}(-$ dbbpy)]2+and $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{d b b p y})]^{+}$ ( $\mathrm{\dbbpy}=2{,}2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dib enzoate) derived UiO-MOF-1 and UiO-MOF-2 to afford $\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}@\$ - MOF-1 and $\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{P}_{2}@\mathsf{M O F}{-}2$ , respectively [97].
Recently, encapsulation of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ complex, [CoII(TPA)Cl][Cl](TP $\mathsf{A}=$ tris(2-pyridylmethyl)amine) into the cages of MOF, MIL-125- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ was achieved via ‘‘ship-in-bottle” approach to afford ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}})$ $\ @\mathrm{MIL}-125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . First, the loading of flexible ligand TPA into the pores of MOF followed by the addition of $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ into the solution afforded the hybrid material. The larger size of cobaloxime compared to the window of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ does not allow cobaloxime to escape from the MOF window (Fig. 18) [168]. A similar study involved the loading of flexible organic molecule $(\mathsf{D O H})_{2}\mathsf{p n}$ within the cavities of MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ with the addition of $\mathsf{C o B r}_{2}$ to the MOF solution to yield cobaloxime $\varpi\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ hybrid [99].
## 3.1.2. Metal loading
Zhen et al. reported the synthesis of MM-MOF with the small sized Ni-particles embedded in the framework of a Zn-based MOF-5. MOF-5 was synthesized solvothermally using $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ - - $6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and BDC in DMF and was added into the solution of Ni $(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ at $60~^{\circ}C$ and allowed to stir for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The reduction using hydrazine provided the precipitates of $\mathsf{N i@}$ MOF-5 [101].
Ti-based MOF was obtained via solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ ions and $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC. Amine functionality can interact with the other metal ions and thus, the transition metal ions $(\mathbf{M}^{2+}=\mathbf{C}\boldsymbol{0}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ) were successfully incorporated into the Ti based MOFs via post synthetic immersion procedure. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis revealed that the $\mathsf{M}^{2+}$ ions coordinated Ti-MOFs possessed octahedron structure even after the coordination of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ ions to Ti based-MOF. This analysis showed the high stability of Ti-MOF against post-synthetic modification (Fig. 19) [102].
Pt NPs were loaded inside the cavities of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based PCN-777 MOF by dispersing the MOF in the aqueous solution of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ followed by reduction in $20\%\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere at $180{\mathrm{~C~}}$ [100]. This approach was also used for the synthesis of Pt-loaded MIL-100 (Fe) using $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ as a reducing agent [103].
Sun et al. synthesized Ag-Gd-MOF by immersing Gd-MOF in MeOH and then, adding solution of ${\sf A g N O}_{3}$ in MeOH dropwise to this with stirring followed by addition of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ that reduced the ${\sf A g}^{+}$ ion to $\mathsf{A g}$ to make it load onto the framework [104].
Polyoxometallates (POMs) based organic frameworks can be modified by loading certain metals into their frameworks. Wang et al. synthesized Ti-based polyoxometallates solvothermally from $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathsf{O B u})_{4}$ , benzoic acid, tetrakis-(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin $(\mathsf{H}_{2^{-}}$ TCPP). This PMOF was then added to a solution of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ with continuous stirring to obtain $\mathsf{P t@P M O F}$ [105].
Wen et al. synthesized Cr-based, amine functionalized $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-101(Cr) MOF which was modified by treating with aqueous $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}$ solution. The amino group interacts with ionic $\mathsf{P t C l}_{4}^{2-}$ resulting into its deposition on the surface of framework followed by the reduction of ionic Pt complex. This leads to the successful deposition of Pt into the framework to form $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{NH_{2}M I L}\ –101$ (Cr) MOF [106].
In a similar study, He et al. modified Zr-based UiO-66 MOF with Pt to form Pt@UiO-66 (Zr) MOF [107]. Fang et al. also utilized the same approach for synthesis of Pt-Al-based MM-MOF [108].
Usually, metal nanoparticles (NPs) are obtained with the help of extra reducing and capping agents. However, Au, Pd, and Pt NPs have also been dispersed on MIL-125(Ti) by an in situ redox reaction between the reductive MIL-125(Ti) with $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ ions and oxidative metal salt precursors without the use of any reducing and capping agents. The synthesized MIL-125(Ti) was added in MeOH and irradiated with light $(\lambda=320–780\ \mathrm{nm})$ which changed the color of the suspension from white to blue due to of the formation of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ by optically induced electron trapping at $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ sites [109].
## 3.2. Metal compound loading (Core shell heterostructured MOFs)
This synthetic approach involves the synthesis of MOFs as a matrix to grow inorganic semiconductor metals usually the metal compound nanoparticles on it through hydrothermal or solvothermal reactions. As the skeleton of MOFs can provide limited space to avoid the growth and agglomeration of NPs, the heterostructured MOFs covered with small sized homodisperse inorganic semiconductor metal compound NPs can be obtained.
Yuan et al. grew CdS NPs upon the surface of UiO-66 MOF by adding MOF and $\mathbf{Cd}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in DMSO and then, heating the mixture at $180{\mathrm{~C~}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ [96]. In a similar approach, $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}@.$ $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ composite was obtained by stirring UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{Cd}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $Z n(0mathsf{A c})_{2}2\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ in a solution of EG with dropwise addition of aqueous solution of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ and the resultant mixture was heated under solvothermal conditions for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $160\mathsf{C}$ [110].
Jiang et al. synthesized UiO-66 from $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and BDC according to the reported method. CdS NPs were grown onto the surface of MOF with $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and thioacetamide (TAA) as cadmium and sulfur sources [94].
Kempe et al. synthesized a photocatalyst composed of MOF core and a gold/anatase shell by obtaining MIL-101(Cr) according to the reported method. This obtained MIL-101(Cr) was kept in [Ti $(\mathrm{OiPr})_{4}]$ at $35\mathrm{{\sfC}}$ under vacuum conditions for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then, the mixture was hydrolyzed under to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ by thermal treatment under $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ atmosphere at $80\mathsf{C}$ and then, followed by calcination at $400\mathbb{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ [111].
A Ni, Pd based $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{CN}/\mathrm{NiPd}$ composite was obtained by stirring $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/CN, Ni and Pd NPs in the methanol solution for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under room temperature conditions [112].
MOFs possess intrinsic porosities and functionalities that make these desirable for various applications. The various reported synthetic approaches for the MM-MOFs have their own significance in enhancement of efficiencies of the materials in a given application via the improvementin synergistic interactions within the metal ions or between the metal ions and organic linkers a. The main advantage of using one pot synthetic approach is that it provides an opportunity to bring different metal ions within the same framework at nodal position. However, this method is associated with a limitation that only electronically/chemically similar metal ions can result into the stable MM-MOFs. The metal ions with different electronic properties and chemical reactivities can be assembled into MMOFs through the metalloligand approach. A metalloligand obtained from one of the metals can be used for the construction of mixed metal-MOFs with one metal ion at nodal position and other at satellite nodal position. The main disadvantage of using metalloligand approach leads to the incorporation of one metal ion at nodal position and the other at satellite nodal position within the frameworks which leads to fickle geometries.
This approach is significant for synthesizing stable heterogeneous systems because the incorporation of metal ions or metalloligands into the frameworks is through the covalent interactions. Coreshell, metal removal-addition and metal replacement approaches are similar to the above discussed approaches in terms of producing chemically stable heterogeneous systems. Loading or metal ions, metal complexes as guests within the frameworks enhances the functionalities for the applicative properties, however, this approach produces less stable heterogeneous systems as the loaded components are connected to each other through noncovalent interactions.
## 4. Applications of MM-MOFs in photocatalytic hydrogen production by water splitting
MM-MOFs are proficient systems for the photocatalytic production of hydrogen from water splitting reactions. However, apart from the indispensable role of the photocatalyst, many other components also play their significant roles in the water splitting reactions. The important components include the sacrificial electron donor (SED), photosensitizer and/or a co-catalyst (Fig. 20). Photosensitizer helps in initiating the reaction by absorbing light energy efficiently to excite the photogenerated electrons from HOMO to LUMO [113,114]. The electron transfer from LUMO to catalytic site takes place either through reductive quench pathway, where the excited photosensitizer is reduced by SED first and then, transfer of electron takes place to the catalytic site; or through an oxidative quench pathway, where the excited photosensitizer transfers electron to the catalytic site first and then, oxidizes the SED afterwards. Sensitizing materials are chosen in such a way that these harvest light in the visible region. Commonly, organic linkers containing metal ions or chromophores of the MOF systems or dyes behave as efficient photosensitizers. Co-catalyst plays its role in transferring electrons from LUMO to the protons by avoiding the recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes. Alkali or transition or inner transition metal ions are mostly found capable for providing the catalytic site for the desirable reaction. These metal orbitals have sufficient energies to tune with organic linkers or other metal orbitals to show various energy transfer mechanisms such as ligand to metal charge transfer (LMCT) or metal to metal charge transfer (MMCT) or metal to ligand charge transfer (MLCT) mechanism which facilitate the electron transfer to the desired catalytic site and promote water splitting reactions. Sacrificial electron donor plays a major role in enhancing hydrogen evolution rate by providing more electron density around holes and avoiding the recombination of charges. Solvent also plays an important role in the photocatalytic process in solubilizing SED and photosensitizer to facilitate the electron transfer mechanisms. Mostly, solvents with more electron density such as $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ MeOH, EtOH or their mixtures are chosen which make them efficient to fill up the holes in the HOMO of the photosensitizer and reduce the recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes. Further, photocatalytic performances of the semiconducting materials are also greatly affected by their optical band gaps as well as BET surface areas.
The components used in the photocatalytic experiment reaction mixture have a strong influence on the stability of frameworks and ultimately, play their role in the recyclability of the MOF. The external and internal environments of the MOFs affect them in terms of chemical stability, mechanical stability, thermal stability and photo stability. Therefore, the stability of the MOF systems is very crucial while choosing these for the photocatalytic study. Mostly, MOFs maintain their structural integrity in water and organic solvents for a long time. However, the introduction of SED such as TEOA, methanol or ascorbic acid into the reaction mixture has an impact on the pH of the mixture where chemically stable MOFs can sustain to those harsh conditions and make themselves durable throughout the photocatalytic study. TEOA can make the pH of the reaction mixture $\sim10$ while ascorbic acid can do it to $\sim3$ . MOFs which are obtained from high valent metal ions (hard Lewis acids) and organic linkers containing carboxylate ions (hard Lewis bases) are unstable in alkali medium and stable in acidic medium. The instability of such systems under high basic conditions can be explained using HSAB rule. Hydroxyl ions are hard bases which can easily replace carboxylate ions containing organic linkers at high pH. Thus, carboxylate coordinated high valent metal organic frameworks are resistant in harsh acidic conditions but, destructive in highly basic conditions. On the other hand, strong interactions exist among azolate based organic linkers (soft Lewis base) and low valent metal ions (soft Lewis acid) according to HSAB principle. These MOFs decompose in acidic solutions and retain their structures in alkali solutions. The mechanism for the removal of azolate based linkers from the MOF systems in acidic medium can be explained using acid-base equilibrium (ABE) theory. Azolate linkers have strong affinity for the protons as they have high pKa values. Thus, azolate linkers combine with protons to leave the azolate linker containing MOF systems under acidic conditions which make them unstable at low pH values and durable at high pH values [42,115-118]. On knowing the reactions conditions of the photocatalytic experiment, stable MOFs can be judicially chosen and vice versa. During the synthesis of MOFs, activation process is a crucial process for their structural and mechanical stability. Mechanically stable MOFs withstand external forces such as high pressure or vacuum conditions during the photocatalytic study. Thus activation of MOFs after its synthesis which has already been discussed in section 2 is a crucial process [119].
The photocatalytic experiment involves the irradiation of MOF system with UV/ Visible light for a long period of time. Here, the photo stability of MOFs has its own significance as organic linkers decompose in the presence of light due to photo-oxidation. The organic molecules upon irradiation with light produces singlet state from triplet state which can attack the double bonds or aromatic rings of the organic components to degrade them. However, locking these organic components in MOF structures improves the photostability of the frameworks. For example porphyrins are photocatalytically unstable, however, locking them into the structures of MOFs by using porphyrins as ligands can make them durable under light conditions [120,121]. Also, the stability of the MOFs under heat conditions without disrupting structure of MOFs makes them thermally stable. MOFs are reported thermally stable from 150 to $500\mathbb{C}.$ Although, the temperature conditions for the desirable experiment is room temperature. So, MOFs with chemical, mechanical, photo stability are thermally stable for the photocatalytic study. The stability factors which are considered for the study of MOFs for the desirable application can in principle be used in designing and choosing the mixed MOFs for photocatalytic application.
Photocatalytic study of a homometallic MOFs is a tedious job because of their limited catalytic efficiencies with large optical band gap. Further, the photocatalytic assembly requires a complex setup consisting of photosensitizer, sacrificial electron donor, cocatalyst and MOF. Alternatively, MM-MOFs provide an ideal platform for the desired process because photosensitizer and catalytic sites can be incorporated within the same photocatalytic system by using a variety of metal ions, photoactive organic linkers or metal complexes. Additionally, the larger BET surface areas and reduced optical band gap with enhanced catalytic properties because of synergistic interactions between metal ions renders MM-MOFs as advanced functional materials for efficient water splitting reactions.
Synthesis of MM-MOFs using various synthetic approaches affects the structural properties of the frameworks to various extents which eventually affects the photocatalytic efficiencies of the frameworks. The photocatalytic activity of these materials synthesized via different approaches is summarized in the sections below.
## 4.1. One-pot synthesized MM-MOFs
MM-MOFs with different metal ions at the nodal positions offer these as excellent materials for the photocatalytic hydrogen production as the synergistic interaction between the metal ions and metal ion-organic linker significantly facilitates the MMCT and LMCT mechanisms. The introduced metal ion in the MOF can act as either an electron sink or an electron mediator to improve the photocatalytic efficiency of the monometallic MOF. Heterogeneity in the frameworks also plays a significant role in reducing their optical band gap, which is another major criterion for the water splitting reactions. The metal orbitals of the bimetallic organic frameworks are reported to be best suited for tuning with the optical properties of the catalyst and also, in enhancing the photogenerated electron-hole recombination time. Meenu et al. reported the unimetallic and bimetallic organic frameworks of cobalt and nickel using BDC and $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC as organic linkers to study photocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water splitting reactions. The concentration of metal ions in the frameworks for the highest electron–hole separation rate and the lowest optical band gap was optimized. The optical band gap was found out to be $2.2~\mathrm{eV}$ at Ni to Co ratio of 1:0.5 and using $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC as an organic linker with HER of $119\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ of hydrogen after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ using trimethylamineacetonitrile $\mathrm{\cdotTEOA:CH_{3}C N}$ mixture as sacrificial electron donor in the presence of sunlight. The enhanced HER of bimetallic MOFs than their corresponding homometallic MOFs $42\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ of hydrogen after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) was attributed to the metal doping and amine functionalized organic linker $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC which behaves as an antenna and absorbs the photons to excite electrons of the organic linker from HOMO to LUMO. The excited photoelectrons in LUMO state produce hydrogen by reacting with hydrogen ions gathered around the cluster and the holes are engaged by electrons of sacrificial agents in HOMO state. Generally, the energy required for ligand to metal charge transfer should be less and negative, however, – $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ group has ELMCT value of $-1.57$ and thus, plays a significant role in reducing the $\mathtt{E_{a b s}}$ from 4.09 to 2.74 making it active in visible region for water splitting reactions [73].
A similar study involved the utilization of Ti-oxo cluster $[\mathrm{Ti}_{8}Z\mathbf{r}_{2}.$ - $)_{12}\:\left(\mathsf{C O O}\right)_{16}\right]$ based PCN-415 and $\mathsf{P C N-}415\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ MOFs to achieve hydrogen evolution rate (HER) of 44 and $594\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}\up h^{-1}$ , respectively using Pt as a co-catalyst. The enhanced HER in case of aminefunctionalized MOF was due to the presence of the amine group which helps in absorbing light to generate photo electron on the Ti site which are then transferred to $\mathrm{Pt}$ to bring out the hydrogen evolution reaction. Ti has been found as an efficient mediator to accept photogenerated electrons from LUMO of ligands and transfer these to the other catalytic site or to the protons to generate hydrogen molecules [122].
A visible light active Co- and Fe-based mixed earth abundant metal–organic framework, $\mathsf{C o_{x}F e_{1-x}}\mathsf{-M O F-74}(0~<~\mathbf{x}~<~1)$ was reported as a promising material for hydrogen evolution from water splitting reactions. A photocatalytic HER of the catalyst was $26{,}715\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ by illuminating it to $450~\mathrm{nm}$ LED lamp and using TEOA as an electron donor and $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer in the mixture of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ for $22\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 21a; c). The prominent reason for hydrogen evolution is attributed to the catalytic active Co and Fe centers which behave as reversible active sites, and also to the accumulation of their reduced forms. The catalytic site which is responsible for hydrogen production is Co where elongated Co-O bond is there. The introduction of Fe center facilitates the elongation of Co-O bond length by reducing the Fe-O bond length. An intermediate species formed under the equilibrium reaction condition with distorted octahedral geometry at Fe center and elongated Co-O bond at the Co center with respect to the original geometry was found. This distortion caused the synergism between the metal ions to bring out the desirable reaction (Fig. 21b) [74]. Such structural synergism was not observed in homometallic MOFs.
While synthesizing the materials for such large-scale applications, cost effectiveness is one of the major parameters to be considered. Keeping this in view, the Na- and Cu-based mixed metal and mixed ligand organic frameworks were reported by Shi et al. In the catalytic system, two Cu ions with same positive charge were in different environment of ligands. $\mathtt{C u}(1)$ existed in a five coordinate square pyramidal geometry, ${\mathrm{Cu}}(2)$ exhibited six coordinate octahedral geometry and, $\mathsf{N a}(1)$ was also in a six coordinate octahedral geometry (Fig. 22a; b). The catalytic system showed HER of $4650~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~{\up g}^{-1}{\hslash}^{-1}$ using trimethylamine (TEA) as an electron donor and fluorescein (FI) as a photosensitizer in $\mathrm{EtOH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ solvent mixture. However, homometallic Cu-MOF showed negligible amount of hydrogen evolution rate. The separation of charges and enhanced transfer process was considered as a plausible mechanism for the hydrogen evolution as confirmed by transient photocurrent and emission quenching experiments. The reaction mechanism involved the transfer of photoexcited electron from FI to the conjugated system of the catalyst where distorted geometry of the metal ions via synergism with each other and evolved hydrogen at Cu sites. TEA played a significant role in recovering FI by sacrificing its electron to fluorescein radical cation (Fig. 22c) [75].
In another study, a $\mathsf{C u}^{\mathrm{{I/II}}}$ -based MM-MOF was reported to show HER of $170\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}$ after $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ of the experiment using Pt as a cocatalyst. The photocatalytic mechanism involved the photo generation of electrons by MOF system which are trapped by Pt to transfer it to the protons for molecular hydrogen generation [76].
Ce-based MOFs have been shown to be the best suited for hydrogen generation from water splitting and their photocatalytic efficiency can be enhanced by making the binary or ternary component systems by combining with other metals. A trimetallic UiO-66 $\mathrm{\langleZr/Ce/Ti\rangle}$ was found to show $210\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ in the visible region using MeOH as an electron donor without the use of any co-catalyst. The enhanced photoactivity of the ternary system was due to the shared cooperation of trimetallic clusters promoting the good charge separation [123].
In a computational study, the use of Zr-Ti doped Ce-based MOF assembled though BDC linker was shown to be a promising material for the water splitting reactions. Kohn-Sham Density Functional Theory (DFT) calculations for the possible synthesis and photocatalytic mechanism for $Z\mathrm{r/Ti}$ modified Ce- based MM-MOF were performed. A close inspection of the most stable doping configurations of $\mathrm{Zr/Ti}$ -Ce-UiO-66 revealed that doping resulted in a negative $\mathsf{E}_{\mathrm{{LMCT}}}\left(-1.43\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ which made it desirable for water splitting reaction by reducing the $\mathtt{E_{a b s}}$ [124].
## 4.2. Metalloligand-based MM-MOFs
Most of the homometallic MOFs are inactive under visible light. However, incorporation of metalloligands into the frameworks can enhance the photocatalytic properties of the MOF materials by making MOFs active under the visible light and facilitating the LMCT mechanism. Ru-, Ir-, Ce or porphyrin-based organic linkers effectively respond to visible light because of their suitable HOMO/LUMO levels and long lived triplet excited state. They can act as light harvesting components within the organic frameworks. Further, Pt or Pd-based organic linkers provide the catalytic sites for the proton reduction mechanism by avoiding electron-hole recombination. These incorporated components provide active sites for the photocatalytic reactions by causing synergistic interactions between these components. On the other hand, if these metalloligands are used as a mixture with homometallic MOFs, no significant enhancement in photocatalytic activity of the MOFs is observed. Their injection into the frameworks cause synergistic interactions between the components and thus, promotes electron transfer mechanism leading to enhanced photocatalytic activities [69].
UiO-67 has an optical band gap of 3.5 which makes it UV active. Several modifications have been reported in the literature to make this MOF active in visible region. One of the reports involves the incorporation of electron deficient $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ half sandwich units into the UiO-67 MOFs. The optical band gap of the obtained $\mathrm{RuOH}_{2}@.$ - UiO mixed MOF is $\sim2.5\mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{RhOH}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO}$ , RuCl@UiO, RhCl@UiO mixed MOFs have $\sim2.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ . This reduction in optical band gap of the mixed MOFs than the pristine MOFs is due to the $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ based metalloligand which shows absorption above $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ and thus, shows metal to ligand charge transfer from $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ metal centers to ligand bpydc. The $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ modified MOFs have the hydrogen evolution TON of 27.6 for RuCl@UiO, 37.4 for RuOH2@UiO, 66.5 for RhCl@UiO and $125.0\mathrm{RhOH}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO}$ using $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ as a photosensitizer, TEOA as a sacrificial electron donor and DMF $/\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ as a solvent. However, the hydrogen evolution TON for UiO is 2.1. Also, the hydrogen evolution TON for the physical mixture of MOF with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ - RuCl is 3.6, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{R u O H}_{2}$ is 5.2, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{R h C l}$ is 12.8 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{RhOH}_{2}$ is 19.3. Thus, simple mixing of MOF with photocatalytic active metalloligands does not play significant role in improving the hydrogen evolution, however, incorporation $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ based metalloligand into the MOFs cause the synergistic interactions between the ligands and metal centers within the frameworks to show enhanced photocatalytic splitting of water. The mechanism involves the harvesting of light by the photosensitizer to go to its excited state which then, transfers photogenerated electrons to $\mathrm{Ru/Rh}$ catalytic centers to transfer them to the protons to produce hydrogen molecules [69].
A Ru-based photosensitizer $[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)(bpy)}_{2}]^{2+}$ and a Pt-based proton reduction catalyst $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ were assembled into the Zrbased MOF to prepare Ru-Pt-Zr-MOF via a one-pot synthesis using the metalloligand (Fig. 23a). Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution of the MM-MOF using EDTA.2Na as an electron donor was found to be $0.5~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ after $^\mathrm{~5~h~}$ of the experiment. The reason for the enhanced photocatalytic activity than its homometallic counterpart was attributed to the uniform distribution of [Ru(dcbpy) $(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}]^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ into the frameworks which results into synergistic interactions between these components. The mechanism involves catering of visible light by Ru complex to transfer photogenerated electron to Pt which avoids electron-hole recombination at an optimum optical band gap and transfers electron to proton to reduce it to the hydrogen molecule. After giving electrons to Pt, Ru complex gets recycled by accepting electron from sacrificial electron donor. The continuous repetition of the catalytic cycle results into the efficient production of hydrogen (Fig. 23b; c) [86].
In another report, upon the incorporation of [Ru(dcbpy) $(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer, $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ as a co-catalyst into UiO-67 MOF, a hydrogen evolution of $34000~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ per gram was achieved using the resulting MM-MOF in N,N-dimethylaniline (DMA), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ mixture after illuminating it with visible light for $30\mathrm{h}$ [125].
In another example, a bipyridine-embedded UiO-type MOF, namely $\mathsf{P t}_{\mathrm{n}}.$ _Ir_BUiO, comprised of $\mathrm{Ir(III)(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{dcbpy)}$ as a photosensitizer, $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})(\mathsf{d c b p y})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ as a molecular catalyst with $\mathrm{TON}_{6}$ .5days of 343 and TOF of $2.9\:\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ [89].
Porphyrins have been reported to behave as redox-active photosensitizers because these have the ability to harvest visible light corresponding to Soret and Q-bands and have long lasting triplet excited state. The lifetimes of Rh-, Pd- and Pt porphyrins at room temperature in their triplet states are in the order of tens to hundreds of microseconds $(\upmu\mathsf{s})$ . A Pd(II) 5,10,15,20- tetrakis(4-carboxy phenyl)porphyrin (PdTCPP) based metalloligand was used to synthesize Pd-PCN-222 (Hf) MM-MOF with BET surface area of 1511 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to show hydrogen evolution rate of $2476\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ using TEOA as an electron donor. The mechanism for the desired reaction involves the harvesting of light by porphyrin based metalloligand to transfer photogenerated electrons to the catalytic site to reduce protons to hydrogen molecules. Enhancement in the hydrogen evolution of mixed MOF was observed on loading of Pt NPs as a co-catalyst onto the frameworks to $22674~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~{\up g^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ because loaded co-catalyst shows the better synergistic interaction with the catalytic centers of the MOF system. Thus, this synergism leads to increases in the photogenerated electron-hole recombination time [126].
A $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -TBP porphyrin based metalloligand was utilized to synthesize MM-MOF with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ru}}_{2}$ based secondary building unit to show hydrogen evolution of $0.24~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ using TEOA as an electron donor in visible light. The reason for the hydrogen generation was attributed to Zn containing porphyrin linker which harvests light to go to its excited state to transfer two electrons to Ru site to form $\mathrm{\bar{\sfRu}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathsf{R u}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ . Further, injection of electrons to Ru site leads to transfer of electron to proton to produce molecular hydrogen. The oxidized $\left(\mathrm{TBP-Zn}\right)^{+}$ is reduced back by SED to complete the redox cycle [88].
Exfoliation of MM-MOFs into nanosheets with efficient hydrogen production in the visible region using $[\mathrm{Ru(tpyCOOH)_{2}}]_{2}$ complex as a linker and transition-metal (Mn, Co, Ni, and $Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}$ ) ions at nodal position was studied by Huo et al. Out of the four metal ions, Ni and Co-based nanosheets were found to show HER of $923\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and $589\upmu\mathrm{mol}\up g^{-1}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively, using ascorbic acid as sacrificial electron donor at $\mathsf{p H}$ of 4.0. The mechanism was reported to involve the photoexcitation of Ru complexed linker to its excited state followed by reduction by sacrificial electron donor. The Ni node accepts the electrons from the reduced ${\mathsf{R}}{\mathsf{u}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ state to transfer them to the protons [84].
Zn-Pd based coordination polymers, $\lfloor Z\mathrm{n}\{\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{IN}\mathrm{A})_{4}\}\rfloor_{\mathrm{n}}$ were found to show photocatalytic hydrogen evolution of $70.6~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in the presence of $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer, methyl viologen $(\mathsf{M V}^{2+})$ and EDTA.2Na as SED [128].
In a similar example, Pd-Zn MOF, $\mathrm{|Zn_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}\{P d C l_{2}(p y d c)\}l_{n},}$ showed hydrogen evolution of $50.0\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in the presence of photochemical set up containing $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M V}^{2+}$ and EDTA.2Na. The enhanced HER was attributed to the active Pd catalytic sites where proton reaction takes place [129].
A $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based UiO-67 MOF showed UV/Vis active hydrogen evolution rate of $26.78\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , however, the performance was enhanced by incorporation of ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ metal ions with 0.02 Ce to $Z\mathrm{r}$ atom ratio via partially replacing bpdc with the dianion of bpydc. The obtained UiO-67-Ce MM-MOF showed the photocatalytic HER of $269.6\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , nearly ten times higher than the parent MOF UiO-67 in an aqueous solution of methanol. This incorporation caused synergistic interaction between metalloligand and metal center of the MOF system and thus, provided an alternative energy transfer pathway between bpdc and bpydc-Ce weakening the LMCT process which involves the transfer of electrons from excited bpdc to zirconium-oxo cluster in UiO-67. This new energy pathway involved the migration of energy from bpdc to bpydc-Ce and it was found effective in suppressing the recombination of photo generated holes and electrons and enhanced the photocatalytic properties (Fig. 24) [87].
## 4.3. Post-modified MM-MOFs
## 4.3.1. Metal replacement (transmetalation)
Ti(IV) based MOFs have great impact in the field of photocatalysis. Sun et al. introduced $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ to the amine functionalized MOF $\left[\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathrm{-UiO}\mathrm{-}66(\mathsf{Z r})\right]$ via post synthetic exchange method to promote electron transfer and improve the HER. $2.4~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ of hydrogen was produced over $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ after irradiating it to visible light for $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ using TEOA as an electron donor. However, this HER was enhanced to $3.5~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ over $\mathrm{Pt/NH_{2}\mathrm{-}U i O\mathrm{-}66(Z r/T i)}$ under same conditions where Ti behaved as a mediator to tune with the optical band gap and enhanced the HER [130]. The synergistic interactions between the metal ions and organic linker improve the LMCT mechanism and thus, resulted into photocatalytic hydrogen evolution that involved the harvesting of light by organic linker to transfer photogenerated electrons to the catalytic site via $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ ions.
## 4.4. Metal, metal complex and metal compound loaded MOFs
## 4.4.1. Metal and metal complex loaded MOFs
HER of MOFs can also be enhanced by loading of either an organometallic complex or a metal onto the surface of frameworks.
These metal NPs or metal complexes behave as co-catalysts which help in transferring electron to the proton by restraining electronhole recombination facilitating the formation of hydrogen molecule. These loaded materials are chosen in such a way that they tune with the band gap of photosensitizing materials and thus, promote the MMCT mechanism. In some cases, loaded components behave as photosensitizers to harvest light in the visible region to transfer photogenerated electrons to the catalytic sites.
Immobilization of POMs into the MOF network can cause the arrangement of polyoxoanions onto the surface of 3D networks. This gives an opportunity to attract cationic photosensitizers (PS) onto itself and thus, causing the synergistic interactions between catalytically active POMs and photosensitizers over the larger surface area of the MOFs. This dispersion of POMs and PS can lead to enhancement of photoactive and catalytic sites on the surface of MOFs and thus, improving the photocatalytic properties of the mixed system than homometallic MOFs. Ni and Co based POMs ${\sf P}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{W}_{17}\mathsf{N i@M I L}\ –101$ and $\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{17}\mathrm{Co@ML}\mathrm{-}101$ was coupled with Cr based MIL-101 MOF along with loading of Ru  polypyridyl based $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ PS to show TON of 50 and 49 for hydrogen evolution from water splitting reaction using TEOA as a sacrificial electron donor, DMF- $\mathrm{\cdot{CH_{3}C N}}$ as a solvent mixture after $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ of the experiment. This strategy was further, used to adsorb anionic $\bar{{\mathsf{M o}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{12}^{2-}}$ and cationic PSs to construct $\mathsf{M o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{12}@\mathsf{M I L}\ –101$ with hydrogen evolution rate of $25587\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (corresponding to $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{S}_{12}^{2-}$ catalyst) after $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ of the experiment under visible light irradiation [132].
An erythrosin B dye-sensitized MIL-101(Cr) coupled with Ni $({\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{mgH}})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(dmgH~=~}$ dimethylglyoxime) complex lead to HER of $45.5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Here, the dye-sensitized MIL-101(Cr) behaved as a light harvester and helped in transferring photogenerated electrons to ${\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{mgH}})_{2}$ complex where proton reduction reaction occurred [133].
In another report, the encapsulation of [Ni(dmobpy) $(2\mathrm{-mpy)_{2}}]$ (dmobpy $\mathit{\Theta}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -dimethoxy- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, $2{\cdot}\mathrm{mpy}=2$ -mercapto pyridyl) complex into Ti-based $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) MOF afforded hydrogen evolution of 2 $\mathrm{{}^{3}\ m o l\ H_{2}\ g(N i)^{-}}$ $_2\mathbf{g}(\mathsf{N i})^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ using TEOA as a sacrificial electron donor. The MOF was proposed to behave as a photo absorber and transferred electron to Ni species to reduce protons to hydrogen molecules [134].
Cobaloxime is a cheap and effective electrocatalysts because of its low over potential. It can be made photoactive by coupling with a photosensitizer. A ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -based complex [CoII(TPA)Cl][Cl] was successfully incorporated inside the cages of Ti-based MIL-125- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ MOF. The larger size of the complex compared to the pores of MOF deformed the MOF structure after the encapsulation and thus, lead to heterogeneity in the frameworks resulting into HER of $553~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~{\up g}^{-1}{\hslash}^{-1}$ using TEOA as an electron donor (Fig. 26b; c; d). Mechanistically, the MOF was proposed to behave as a light harvester to generate photoelectrons that were transferred to Cocomplex where reduction of proton to hydrogen molecules occurred by tuning the optical band gap of the MOF system (Fig. 26a).
Cobaloxime based catalyst $\mathrm{Co(dmgH)_{2}(4\mathrm{-}H E P)C l}$ $.4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{HEP}=4$ -(2- hydroxyethyl)pyridine] was also immobilized on a chromium terephthalate MOF, MIL-101(Cr) to construct heterogeneous system that resulted into the hydrogen evolution of $1.5\mathrm{\mmol\h}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ using TEOA as an electron donor and eosin-Y(EY) as a photosensitizer [135].
Further, encapsulation of cobaloxime based proton reduction catalyst within the light harvesting Ti based $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ – MIL-125(Ti) MOF provided TOF of $0.8\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ after $65\mathrm{~h~}$ of the operation using $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer and without use of any noble metal as a co-catalyst. Loading of complex enhanced the activity by a factor of 20 from pristine MOF due to amine functionalized organic linkers, Ti based clusters which are photo efficient to tune with the catalytic site [99].
A high-nuclear $\{\mathsf{C u}_{24}^{\mathrm{I}}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{C l})_{8}(\upmu_{4}{-}\mathsf{C l})_{6}\}$ -based POMs $({\mathsf{P O M}}){\widehat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}}$ MOF (ZZULI-1) was rationally designed for the production of both hydrogen and oxygen from water splitting reactions. A high HER of $6614~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ was obtained using TEA as a sacrificial electron donor and FI dye as a photosensitizer. The mechanistic pathway involved the generation of photogenerated electrons by FI to transfer it to the Cu based catalytic site through MOF where production of molecular hydrogen takes place and TEA continues the photocatalytic cycle by sacrificing the electrons to FI (Fig. 27) [136].
[FeFe] hydrogenases $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{s}\mathbf{e}\mathbf{s}\right)$ have been found as attractive organocomplex to load into the MOF because these provide the firm catalytic sites for the proton reduction reaction. A study involved the coupling of $(\mathrm{[FeFe](dcbdt)(CO)_{6}}$ , dcbdt $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}1,4-$ dicarboxylbenzene-2,3-ditiolate) with robust $Z\mathrm{{r}(\mathrm{{IV})}}.$ -based MOF leading to and enhanced HER using ascorbate as a SED and $[\mathsf{R}\mathsf{u}$ $(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ as a photosensitizer at pH of 5 [137]. Wu et al. reported a [FeFe]- ${\bf\cdot H}_{2}\mathsf{a s e s}$ with Gd-coupled TCA MOF for hydrogen generation using $\mathsf{N i P r}_{2}\mathsf{E t H}$ OAc $(0.8~\mathrm{M})$ as a SED in the presence of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ - $C N\mathrm{-}H_{2}O$ solvent. TOF of Gd-TCA for hydrogen evolution was enhanced to $320~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ by encapsulation of $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]\ \mathsf{C l}_{2}$ using TEA as a SED at pH of 10.0 [138].
4.4.1.2. Metal loaded MOFs. Loading of noble metal nanoparticles onto the surface of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF UiO-66(Zr) improved the photocatalytic properties of framework by promoting the electron transfer and restraining the charge recombination. Notably, UiO-66(Zr)
itself does not absorb visible light for the production of hydrogen from water. He et al. adsorbed visible light active dye RhB onto the surface of $\operatorname*{Pt}(\mathbf{\widehat{\mu}})$ UiO-66(Zr) that improved the HER to1 $16.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ . The main reason for the improvement in HER was attributed to the absorption of visible light by RhB (photosensitizer) to give excited $\mathrm{RhB^{*}}$ which provided electrons to the LUMO of the framework to evolve hydrogen. Pt was reported to play a significant role in transferring electrons to the protons by avoiding photogenerated charge recombination [107].
Sensitization of Pt-loaded $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ MOF with a cone-calix [4]arene dye Calix-3 with four D- $\cdot\pi$ -A units resulted into a significant enhancement in the photoactivity and the HER $1528\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ - $\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ achieved that was much higher than $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ ( $\mathrm{\:\:HER=}$ $220\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{,}$ [139].
A Pt-loaded ultrathin porphyrin-based MOF with Ti-oxo clusters was explored as an efficient, visible light active catalyst with HER 8.52 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ from water using ascorbic acid as an electron donor. In the resulting PMOF, porphyrin $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T C P P})$ acted as a light harvester and transferred electron to Ti-oxo clusters which further, transferred the electrons to overlapped Pt nanoparticles where the proton reduction occurred. The LMCT was proposed to be the major pathway in the process (Fig. 28). These Ti-oxo clusters were synthesized by reacting $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathsf{O B u})_{4}$ with benzoic acid solvothermally followed by their reaction with ${\sf H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ The advantage of using porphyrin based MOF is that it provides a larger surface area for the photocatalytic reaction. (Figs. 28 and 29) [105].
Using a similar approach, encapsulation of Pt nanoparticles into Cr-based amine functionalized MOF $[\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Cr)] makes the photocatalytic activity of the material to $28.6~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ with TEOA as an electron donor, RhB as a photosensitizer and Pt as a catalyst. In the proposed mechanism, harvesting of light by RhB followed by an electron transfer from MOF to Pt nano particles and proton reduction caused the hydrogen generation [106].
Dispersion of noble metals as a catalyst over the surface of MOFs also improves the photocatalytic activity of the frameworks to several times; however, obtaining a uniform and size controlled dispersion remains a challenge. In a report, Fang et al. successfully obtained a single atom Pt dispersion confined onto the surface of the Al-based porphyrinic MOF formulated as Al-TCPP. In a 3D microporous framework, large $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})\mathsf{O}_{4}$ chains were interconnected using porphyrin linkers and Pt atoms were stabilized by their interactions with pyrrolic N atoms. Pt atoms were incorporated by employing $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})$ into the porphyrin centers followed by the reduction. The so obtained heterogeneous framework exhibited the HER of $129~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~{\up g}^{-1}{\hslash}^{-1}$ employing TEOA as an electron donor in visible light in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ . The recombination of charges resulting into the lower HER for Al-TCPP $(\mathrm{HER}=1.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ could thus be avoided by incorporating Pt NPs to form Al-TCPPPtNPs (Fig. 30) [108].
Polypyridyl $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})$ complex is a homogenous photocatalyst for water splitting reaction with significant efficiency. However, poor recyclability because of homogenous behavior limits its application. This issue could be resolved by incorporating this complex onto the surface of a MOF to make it heterogeneous system. UiO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ was used as a stable platform to incorporate the metal complex and hydrogen evolution of $56~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ could be achieved after $^{9\mathrm{{h}}}$ of the experiment using 0.1 M EDTA as an electron donor [140].
A triazine-based MOF synthesized from Co and 2,4,6-Tris(4-car boxyphenyl)-1,3,5-triazine $\left(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A T B}\right)$ , denoted as PCN-9(Co), was reported to have poor photocatalytic activity for hydrogen generation from water splitting. However, Pt complex encapsulated, PCN9-Pt MM-MOF exhibited an improved activity with HER $33.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\up g^{-1}\up h^{-1}$ . The enhancement in HER was due to the minimization of the recombination of charges by Pt ions in the MMMOF [141].
In another report, encapsulation of $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})$ complex into the dcbpy derived Zr-MOF afforded the visible light active hydrogen evolution of $8.3\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ after the $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ reaction using TEOA as an electron donor [142]. A heterogeneous system containing a MOF, PCN777 and Pt catalyst showed an enhanced HER of $586~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~{\up g^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ [100].
Improvement of photoactivity of MOFs upon loading of noble NPs is also greatly influenced by the location of these NPs inside the frameworks. In a study, encapsulation of Pt NPs inside UiO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ MOF and supported on the surface of the MOF, denoted as $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ , respectively resulted into better HER than the pristine MOF. $\mathrm{Pt/UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ was obtained by reaction of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ salt with $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC and later Pt NPs were grown in-situ via one-pot synthesis. The water splitting reaction yielded a HER of $50.26~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\up g^{-1}\up h^{-1}$ . $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ was synthesized by reacting $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ salt and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC solvothermally followed by deposition of Pt NPs with hydrogen evolution rate of $257.38~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ - $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ (Fig. 31). The $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ greatly contracts the optical band gap and thereby, has much higher efficiency than Pt/UiO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ [143].
Fabrication of MIL-125(Ti) with highly dispersed Pt nanoparticles without employing any reducing and capping agents resulted into hydrogen evolution of $38.68\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ after $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ using TEOA as sacrificial electron donor in case of Pt deposition. Here, pristine MOF containing the cyclic octamers of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ octahedra provided extra stability to the frameworks to enhance its reusability. Moreover, NPs were deposited onto the frameworks without use of any reducing agents which limits the introduction of any impurities inside the frameworks [109].
Photo deposition of Pt NPs on the amine functionalized Ti based MOF, named $\mathsf{P t/T i-M O F-N H_{2}}$ , was studied to enhance photocatalytic performance with hydrogen evolution of $11.7~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ Mechanistically, the photocatalytic performance was majorly attributed to ligand to cluster charge transfer (LCCT). The amine group behaved as an antenna for the efficient absorption of visible light to go to the excited state and then transfer electrons to Pt. Further, Pt avoids recombination of charges and produce hydrogen molecules by transferring electrons from cluster to protons (Fig. 32) [144].
A MM-MOF obtained by the dispersion of Pt NPs on the MIL-100 (Fe) exhibited HER of $109\ \mathrm{\upmumol\g^{-1}h^{-1}}$ . Similar to the previous studies, Pt played an important role in trapping of electrons and their transfer to the protons for the formation of molecular hydrogen [103].
Undoubtedly, loading of noble metal nanoparticles on MOF systems enhance their photocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water splitting reactions by promoting a synergism in the resulting MMMOFs, however, low abundance and the high cost limit their availability in the practical applications. Therefore, non-noble metal nanoparticles have been successfully explored for coupling with MOF systems. In one such example, the deposition of small sized Ni NPs on the surface of MOF-5 showed a photocatalytic hydrogen evolution rate of $30.22~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [Ni] using TEOA as an electron donor and EY as a photosensitizer at $\mathsf{p H}=11$ . The hydrogen evolution of pristine MOF sensitized with EY was $9.67~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ of irradiation which reflects that metal loading has a significant role in enhancing hydrogen evolution rate. The large
BET surface area of MOF-5 $(2973~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ allows the access of guest molecules onto its surface. The mechanism of hydrogen evolution is similar to the mechanism as discussed in case of loading of Pt NPs. Ni NPs evolves hydrogen by trapping electrons from photosensitizer and transferring them to the protons for reduction to molecular hydrogen [145].
An aluminum-based MOF, Al-ATA prepared from 2- aminoterephthalate, evolved oxygen from water splitting reaction, however, decoration of MOF with $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ cations resulted into the hydrogen evolution from water splitting reaction indicating the hydrogen generation at Ni active sites. HER activity of $36.0\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ was achieved using $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ as a sacrificial electron donor (Fig. 33d). XANES and extended EXAFS measurements showed the $^+2$ oxidation state and octahedral coordination of Ni ion in the framework. (Fig. 33a-c) [146].
In another such study, the photo deposition of Ni NPs on MIL101 MOF produced hydrogen with HER of $125\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ using Erythrosin B dye as a photosensitizer [147].
Ag NPs were also successfully decorated on the dye-like azocarboxylic acid based Gd-MOF to obtain hydrogen evolution of $10.6\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Hydrogen evolved at Ag NPs through their trapping of electrons from MOF [104].
A Ni- and Mo- derived cluster anchored to the surface of highly conductive MIL-100 with BET surface area of $3361~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}~\mathrm{{g}}^{-1}$ displayed HER of $1480.4\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in 120 min using (EY) as a photosensitizer. (EY) harvest visible light to form excited $\mathrm{EY^{*}}$ which transfers electrons to the MOF system that further transfers electrons to NiMo alloy system where proton reduction takes place [148].
Immensely, the photoactivity of MOFs has been tuned to visible light by sensitizing them with visible active dyes or by the incorporation of photosensitizers into the frameworks. However, decoration of MOFs with metal NPs which behave as plasmons, offers an alternative approach for making the frameworks photoactive in visible light. A Pt@MOF/Au photocatalyst integrating two types of metal-MOF interfaces i.e. the surface plasmon resonance excitation of Au nanorods and Pt-MOF Schottky junction, accelerated the charge transfer mechanism providing a visible light active material. Thus, Pt@MOF/Au displayed enhanced photocatalytic HER of $1743.0\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , superior to the Pt/MOF/Au, MOF/Au MMOF and other counterparts (Fig. 34). The proposed mechanism favored the synergism between the plasmonic Au NPs which (absorbing visible light to transfer electrons to the MOF system) and the Pt Schottky junction (takes electrons from MOF system to reduce protons) for the efficient hydrogen generation [149].
A $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{b p y d c})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ loaded Al derived MOF-253 obtained by post synthetic modification showed an enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution in visible light with an amount of $\sim2400\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ using TEOA as an electron donor, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N-H_{2}O}$ as a solvent at an optimum $\mathsf{p H}8.5$ . The loading of $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{b p y d c})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ complex was confirmed by BET studies and BET surface area of MOF-253-Pt was reduced $(527\ m^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1})$ compared to that of MOF-253 ( $1237~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ . However, the so obtained photocatalyst does not require any co-catalyst or a photosensitizer and exhibited a photocatalytic activity five times higher than that of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -complex. X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy and low temperature luminescence studies revealed the presence of short Pt-Pt interactions that were considered to be responsible for the enhanced photo activity [150].
In a computational study, Zhong et al. modified Ti-based MOF with Pt nanoparticles. The DFT calculations performed using $\mathrm{Pt}/20\%$ -MIL-125- $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathsf{S C H}_{3}).}\end{array}$ predicted the higher photocatalytic efficiency of the MM-MOF with NPs than the MOF without NPs. The main reason was attributed to the lower over potential of the Pt NPs to accumulate protons around these particles and thus avoid the charge recombination leading to evolve hydrogen with full efficiency [151].
## 4.4.2. Core-shell heterostructured MOFs
Various semiconductor materials such as metal nanoparticles, metal oxides, metal sulfides, and metal phosphides have been reported to show enhanced photocatalytic activity in visible region. However, these are associated with the limitation of fast photogenerated electron-hole recombination rate and high agglomeration rate because of their large particle size. Also, most of the MOFs are inactive under visible light. Such discrepancies can be avoided by the dispersion of these materials onto the surface of MOFs having large surface area, multiple functionalities and tunable porosities. This results in enhancing the photocatalytic activity of the hybrid materials by combining the advantages of harvesting light in visible region and more catalytic sites for the desired reaction at one place through synergistic interactions [94,152].
A core–shell designed up-conversion nanoparticles composite, (UCNPs)-Pt@MOF/Au was developed for photocatalytic hydrogen generation. Here, the MOF showed UV and little visible light activity. The plasmonic Au NPs harvested visible light and thus, UCNPs harvested NIR light to emit UV and visible light that are absorbed by the MOF and Au again (Fig. 35). MOF not only provided the required access for catalytic surface to Pt active sites, but also facilitates the generation of clean Au NPs on its surface and ensured the spatial separation of the Au and Pt NPs. The resulting optimized core–shell composite showed photocatalytic HER of $280\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}$ - $\ensuremath{\mathbf{h}}^{-1}$ [153].
4.4.2.1. Metal sulfides decorated heterostructured MOFs. CdS has been reported for the photocatalytic production of hydrogen from water splitting reactions because of their activity in visible light. In a report the incorporation of CdS onto the surface of UiO-66 MOF improved hydrogen evolution to $235\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ using ascorbic acid as an electron donor. UiO-66 has the BET surface area of $987.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ which is large enough to incorporate CdS onto its surface to form composite with BET surface area of $539.6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . It has been found that reported physical mixture of CdS and MOF can make the system to show hydrogen evolution of $40.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . This shows that causing the heterogeneity by incorporating CdS onto the surface of MOF system is the main reason for the enhanced hydrogen evolution rate. This heterogeneity can efficiently cause synergistic interaction between CdS and MOF system to transfer photogenerated electrons from the conduction band of CdS to the conduction band of MOF system at the interfacial level of the two components. These transferred photogenerated electrons are then, transferred to protons to produce hydrogen. The holes which remain at the valence level of the CdS are filled by ascorbic acid [96].
In one another report, a CdS decorated UiO-66 MOF composite exhibited hydrogen production rate of $1725\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{S}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ using lactic acid as a sacrificial electron donor in the visible region with acetonitrile as a solvent. The reason for the efficient hydrogen production was attributed to the synergistic interactions between CdS and MOF that facilitated the electron transfer mechanism for the hydrogen production at the interfacial level by increasing the catalytic sites and suppressing the recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs [94].
Synergistic interaction between CdS, UiO-66 and rGO to enhance the photocatalytic hydrogen production of the system to $13.8\mathrm{\mmol}\mathrm{\g}_{\mathrm{CdS}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The mechanism for the hydrogen production involved thetransfer of photogenerated electrons from conduction band of CdS to the fermi level of rGO via MOF system and then, to the protons [152].
Notably, Pt has been reported as the best co-catalyst for the hydrogen evolution from water splitting reaction. However, its high cost and rarity poses an implication to use it on commercial level. Various semiconductor metal sulfides such as $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ have been found to be efficient replacements of Pt. One such study involved the formation of ternary composite of UiO-66, CdS and $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ resulting into the hydrogen evolution rate of $650~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . CdS being visible light active harvest light in the visible region and generate photo electrons at conduction band to transfer them to the MOF where, $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ accepts the photo electrons from UiO-66 to transfer further to the protons to produce hydrogen [154].
UiO-66 MOF is inactive in visible light, however, incorporation of amine groups into the frameworks can improve its photoactivity in visible region because of its negative ELMCT value and thus, reducing the band gap. In a study, a visible light active solid solution of CdS and ZnS, $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ was decorated onto the surface of UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the resulting composite exhibited hydrogen production rate of $5846.5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The remarkable hydrogen evolution of the composite was reasoned to be due to the harvesting of visible light by both MOF and $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ where, photogenerated electrons from the conduction band of the MOF get transferred to the conduction band of the $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ at the interfacial level of the composite. Protons from water splitting reactions accept these transferred electrons to produce molecular hydrogen [110].
4.4.2.2. Metal phosphides decorated heterostructured MOFs. A noblemetal-free transitional metal phosphides (TMPs, e.g., ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}$ $\mathsf{N i}_{12}\mathsf{P}_{5}$ ), are incorporated into a MOF, ${\mathrm{UiO}}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ , to be used as co-catalyst for the photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production with hydrogen evolution rate of $409.1\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $293.2\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ respectively. Also, Pt loaded $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ showed hydrogen evolution rate of $378.0\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .From the thermodynamic and kinetic studies, TMPs are found to show similar behaviour to Pt by accelerating linker to cluster charge transfer and promoting the charge separation and thus, reducing the activation energy of hydrogen production. However, Pt is thermodynamically favorable and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ is kinetically favorable for the hydrogen production because of the higher activity of the higher activity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@U i O-66-N H_{2}}$ than $\mathsf{P t@U i O{-}G6{-}N H_{2}}$ [155].
4.4.2.3. Metal oxides decorated heterostructured MOFs. A Cr based MIL-101 PCP/MOF has been reported to be used as a core to grow crystalline anatase as a shell on itself with plasmonic gold NPs. This ternary composite combines the plasmonic effect of gold NPs with semiconducting properties of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs and MOF. The obtained hybrid system shows photocatalytic hydrogen evolution of $903\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ using MeOH as a sacrificial electron donor in the visible region [111].
