## 5. Conclusion and perspectives
The effective harnessing of the renewable energy is the global priority to meet the multiplying energy demands. Among a variety of materials explored for the hydrogen generation from water splitting reactions, MOFs with high stability, ordered structures and high surface areas exhibited limited catalytic efficiencies in visible region for such reactions. However, the introduction of heterogeneity within the frameworks by either incorporating the metal ions at the nodal positions of the frameworks or by loading metal ions/ metal complexes/ metallic compounds in the frameworks has emerged as an effective strategy towards the improvement of photocatalytic activity for such reactions. The presence of synergistic interactions between metals ions as well as the assembly of multiple components such as photosensitizer, catalytic sites etc. in a single heterogeneous framework render these materials as potent photocatalysts for water splitting process for the efficient hydrogen production. In this article, the literature on the synthetic approaches for various MM-MOFs have been reviewed along with their applications in the hydrogen production by water splitting reactions, and the mechanisms of action.In summary, the MM-MOFs obtained via one-pot synthesis using various metal ions shows enhanced photocatalytic efficiency than their homometallic parts is due to the collective cooperation of the metal ions through organic linkers. Introduction of new metal ion at the nodal position of the homometallic MOF system distorts the geometry of the organic frameworks at the catalytic sites. This distortion causes the synergistic interaction between the metal orbitals and enhances the catalytic properties. Further, the introduction of photoactive organic linkers containing chromophores such as $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ group or photoactive organometallic complexes of Ru, Ir, Ce etc. enhances the visible light activity of the homometallic frameworks by promoting the electron transfer mechanism. In addition, loading of metals, metal nanoparticles or metal complexes into the frameworks increases the charge recombination time as the loaded materials can tune the frameworks to behave as electron sink and transfer the electrons efficiently to the protons. The complexes of Ni, Co, Pt metals and based nanoparticles of Pt, Ni, Au, Ag, Pd metals have shown proficient tuning with metal orbitals of the MOF systems leading to the enhanced photocatalytic activity of these materials in water splitting reactions for hydrogen generation. A very rapid progress in this area in recent years offers new opportunities towards the development of highly efficient materials for the photocatalytic hydrogen production from water. Development of effectual synthetic methods for well-organized MM-MOFs, mixed MOF composites and the tuning of their photo-catalytic activities for water splitting in the visible region are the major research foci at present. Despite significant research, there still exist some challenges which needs to overcome such as, (i) a number of rare and costly metal (Pt, Ir, Ru etc.) based NPs, complexes, metalloligands are used as photosensitizers and co-catalysts in the photocatalytic study which need to be replaced with efficient earth abundant metal based complexes or metalloligand to commercialize these studied mixed MOFs (ii) a number of synthetic approaches have been designed to incorporate photosensitizing and catalytic sites in one framework, however, the stability of these heterogeneous systems is a challenge. The focus of the future research should be in designing the stable mixed MOFs with photosensitizing and catalytic centers at one place (iii) Sacrificial electron donors are used separately in the photocatalytic experiments, however, their cost is very high which is a challenge. Future research should be in designing mixed MOFs which can fulfill the role of sacrificial electron donor too.
A very rapid progress in this area in recent years offers new opportunities towards the development of highly efficient materials for the photocatalytic hydrogen production from water. The progression of MM-MOFs has remarkably improved the efficiency of MOFs in the water splitting reactions for hydrogen production. Careful design of the metals and ligands as well as the synthetic approach is amenable to the practical applications of MM-MOFs in hydrogen production.
